  "id": "Compound::DB00758 - Gene::1557",
  "verbalization": "Clopidogrel, a thienopyridine antiplatelet agent, is linked to Cyp2c19 through multiple pathways that converge on the mechanism of action involving the P2y12 receptor and the effects of thienopyridine on platelet function. The evidence suggests that Clopidogrel, through its inclusion in the class of thienopyridine antiplatelet agents, interacts with Cyp2c19 via the binding of Ticlopidine, a related drug in the same class, which in turn binds to Cyp2c19. This indicates a potential shared metabolic pathway or enzyme interaction between Clopidogrel and Ticlopidine, both of which are thienopyridine derivatives. The lowest common ancestor of Clopidogrel and Ticlopidine points to the thienopyridine antiplatelet agent class, suggesting a common functional or pharmacological relationship. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Cyp2c19 in the metabolism of thienopyridine drugs, possibly through the cytochrome P450 system, and underscores the importance of this enzyme in the pharmacological effects of these agents.",
